# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
EN = {}
EN[124] = "Oksana stood a long time, pondering the blacksmith's strange words. Already something inside her told her that she had treated him too cruelly. <<OKSANA>>“What if he really decides on something terrible? Heaven knows! Perhaps in his grief he will take it into his head to fall in love with another and out of spite will call her the first beauty in the village? But no, he loves me. I am so pretty! He will not exchange me for anything; he is playing, pretending. Not ten minutes will pass before he surely comes to look at me. I really am harsh. I must let him kiss me, as if unwillingly. How glad he will be!”<<N>> And the flighty beauty was already joking with her friends. <<GIRLS>>“Wait,”<<N>> said one of them, <<GIRLS>>“the blacksmith has forgotten his sacks; look: what terrible sacks! He did not carol as we do: I think they have thrown a whole quarter of a ram into them; and the sausages and loaves surely cannot be counted. Luxury! You could gorge on them for whole holidays.”"
EN[125] = "<<OKSANA>>“Are these the blacksmith's sacks?”<<N>> Oksana caught up. <<OKSANA>>“Let us drag them quickly to my cottage and look properly at what he has packed in here.”<<N>> All approved this proposal with laughter."
EN[126] = "<<GIRLS>>“But we can't lift them!”<<N>> the whole crowd cried at once, trying to move the sacks."
EN[127] = "<<OKSANA>>“Wait,”<<N>> said Oksana, <<OKSANA>>“let us run quickly for a sledge and take them on the sledge!”"
EN[128] = "And the crowd ran for a sledge."
EN[129] = "The prisoners were greatly tired of sitting in the sacks, although the deacon had poked a fair hole for himself with his finger. If there had been no people about, he might have found a means of climbing out; but to climb out of the sack before everyone, to show himself to be laughed at... this held him back, and he decided to wait, only coughing a little under Chub's impolite boots. Chub himself wished no less for freedom, feeling that under him lay something on which it was terribly uncomfortable to sit. But as soon as he heard his daughter's decision he calmed down and no longer wanted to climb out, reasoning that to reach his cottage he had to go at least a hundred paces, perhaps two hundred. And once out, he would have to tidy himself, button his sheepskin, tie his belt: so much work! and besides, his cap had been left at Solokha's. Better let the girls bring him on the sledge. But it turned out quite differently from what Chub expected: at the time the girls ran for the sledge, the lean friend was coming out of the tavern, upset and out of sorts. The tavern-keeper would not on any account trust him with credit; he had meant to wait in case some pious gentleman came and treated him; but, as if on purpose, all the gentlemen stayed at home and, as honest Christians, were eating kutya in the midst of their families. Reflecting on the corruption of manners and on the wooden heart of the Jewess who sold wine, the friend came upon the sacks and stopped in astonishment. <<PANAS>>“Look what sacks someone has thrown down in the road!”<<N>> he said, looking about on all sides. <<PANAS>>“There must be pork here, too. Somebody was lucky to collect so many things by carolling. What terrible sacks! Suppose they are stuffed only with buckwheat cakes and flat cakes, even that is good. Even if there were only loaves here, that would be tasty: the Jewess gives a glass of vodka for each loaf. Drag them off quickly, so that no one sees.”<<N>> Then he heaved onto his shoulders the sack with Chub and the deacon, but felt that it was too heavy. <<PANAS>>“No, it will be hard to carry alone,”<<N>> he said, <<PANAS>>“but here, as if on purpose, comes the weaver Shapuvalenko. Good evening, Ostap!”"
EN[130] = "<<WEAVER>>“Good evening,”<<N>> said the weaver, stopping."
EN[131] = "<<PANAS>>“Where are you going?”"
EN[132] = "<<WEAVER>>“Oh, just walking where my feet take me.”"
EN[133] = "<<PANAS>>“Help me carry the sacks, good man! Someone was carolling and threw them down in the middle of the road. We will share them in friendship, half and half.”"
EN[134] = "<<WEAVER>>“Sacks? And what is in the sacks, rolls or loaves?”"
EN[135] = "<<PANAS>>“I think there is everything.”<<N>> Then they quickly pulled some sticks out of a wattle fence, laid the sack on them, and carried it on their shoulders."
EN[136] = "<<WEAVER>>“Where shall we carry it? To the tavern?”<<N>> asked the weaver on the way."
EN[137] = "<<PANAS>>“I thought so myself, to the tavern; but the accursed Jewess will not believe it, she will even think we stole it somewhere; besides, I have only just come from the tavern. We will carry it to my cottage. No one will disturb us: my wife is not at home.”"
EN[138] = "<<WEAVER>>“But is she really not at home?”<<N>> asked the careful weaver."
EN[139] = "<<PANAS>>“Thank God, we are not quite out of our wits yet,”<<N>> said the friend. <<PANAS>>“Would the devil bring me where she is? She will drag about with the women till daylight, I think.”"
EN[140] = "<<WIFE>>“Who is there?”<<N>> cried the friend's wife, hearing the noise in the porch made by the arrival of the two companions with the sack, and opening the door."
EN[141] = "The friend stood rooted to the spot."
EN[142] = "<<WEAVER>>“Well, there you are!”<<N>> said the weaver, letting his arms fall."
EN[143] = "The friend's wife was the sort of treasure of which there are not a few in the world. Like her husband she almost never sat at home and crawled about almost the whole day among her gossips and well-to-do old women, praising and eating with great appetite, and fought only in the mornings with her husband, because only at that time did she sometimes see him. Their cottage was twice as old as the district clerk's trousers: in some places the roof had no thatch. Of the fence only remnants were visible, because everyone who left the house never took a stick for the dogs, in the hope that he would pass the friend's vegetable garden and pull one out of his fence. The stove was not lit for days at a time. Everything the tender wife begged from good people she hid as far as possible from her husband, and often forcibly took away his booty if he had not managed to drink it away at the tavern. The friend, in spite of his constant coolness, did not like to give way to her; and so he almost always left the house with lanterns under both eyes, and his dear half, groaning, trailed off to tell the old women about her husband's outrages and the beatings she had suffered from him."
EN[144] = "Now one can imagine how the weaver and the friend were taken aback by such an unexpected appearance. Putting down the sack, they stood in front of it and covered it with the skirts of their coats; but it was too late: the friend's wife, although she saw badly with her old eyes, noticed the sack all the same. <<WIFE>>“That is good!”<<N>> she said with a look in which the joy of a hawk could be seen. <<WIFE>>“It is good that you have collected so much by carolling! That is how good people always do; but no, I think you have picked it up somewhere. Show me at once, do you hear, show me your sack this minute!”"
EN[145] = "<<PANAS>>“The bald devil will show you, not we,”<<N>> said the friend, drawing himself up."
EN[146] = "<<WEAVER>>“What business is it of yours?”<<N>> said the weaver. <<WEAVER>>“We collected it, not you.”"
EN[147] = "<<WIFE>>“No, you will show me, worthless drunkard!”<<N>> cried the wife, striking the tall friend with her fist on the chin and pushing through to the sack. But the weaver and the friend bravely defended the sack and made her fall back. They had hardly recovered when the wife ran out into the porch, already with a poker in her hands. Quickly she struck her husband on the hands with the poker, the weaver on the back, and was already standing beside the sack."
EN[148] = "<<WEAVER>>“Why did we let her in?”<<N>> said the weaver, coming to his senses."
EN[149] = "<<PANAS>>“Oh, why did we let her! And why did you let her!”<<N>> said the friend coolly."
EN[150] = "<<WEAVER>>“Your poker must be iron!”<<N>> said the weaver after a short silence, scratching his back. <<WEAVER>>“My wife bought a poker last year at the fair; gave half a kopeck for it; and that one is nothing... it doesn't hurt...”"
EN[151] = "Meanwhile the triumphant wife, setting a little lamp on the floor, untied the sack and looked into it."
EN[152] = "But surely her old eyes, which had seen the sack so well, were deceived this time. <<WIFE>>“Oh, there is a whole boar lying here!”<<N>> she cried, clapping her hands with joy."
EN[153] = "<<WEAVER>>“A boar! Do you hear, a whole boar!”<<N>> the weaver nudged his friend. <<WEAVER>>“And it is all your fault!”"
EN[154] = "<<PANAS>>“What is to be done!”<<N>> said the friend, shrugging his shoulders."
EN[155] = "<<WEAVER>>“What do you mean, what? Why are we standing here? Let us take the sack away! Come on, attack!”"
EN[156] = "<<WEAVER>>“Get away! Away! This is our boar!”<<N>> shouted the weaver, stepping forward."
EN[157] = "<<PANAS>>“Go, go, you devil's woman! This is not your property!”<<N>> said the friend, coming closer."
EN[158] = "The wife took up the poker again, but at that moment Chub climbed out of the sack and stood in the middle of the porch, stretching like a man who had just woken from a long sleep."
EN[159] = "The friend's wife cried out, striking her hands against her skirts, and everyone involuntarily gaped."
EN[160] = "<<PANAS>>“What is she saying, the fool: a boar! This is not a boar!”<<N>> said the friend, goggling."
EN[161] = "<<WEAVER>>“Look what a man has been thrown into the sack!”<<N>> said the weaver, backing away in fright. <<WEAVER>>“Say what you like, burst if you will, but there is the unclean power in this. He would not even fit through the window!”"
EN[162] = "<<PANAS>>“It is friend Chub!”<<N>> cried the friend, looking closer."
EN[163] = "<<CHUB>>“And who did you think it was?”<<N>> said Chub, smiling. <<CHUB>>“Well, what a fine trick I played on you! And you, I dare say, wanted to eat me instead of pork? Wait, then, I will please you: there is something else in the sack, if not a boar then surely a piglet or some other creature. Something was moving under me all the time.”"
EN[164] = "The weaver and the friend rushed to the sack, the lady of the house seized it from the opposite side, and the fight would have broken out again if the deacon, seeing now that he had nowhere to hide, had not scrambled out of the sack."
EN[165] = "The friend's wife, rooted to the spot, let go of the leg by which she had begun to pull the deacon out of the sack."
EN[166] = "<<WEAVER>>“Here is another one!”<<N>> cried the weaver in fear. <<WEAVER>>“The devil knows how things are in the world... my head is spinning... not sausages and loaves, but people are thrown into sacks!”"
EN[167] = "<<CHUB>>“It is the deacon!”<<N>> said Chub, more astonished than anyone. <<CHUB>>“Well, there you are! Good for Solokha! To put him in a sack... So that is why I saw her cottage full of sacks... Now I know everything: she had two men sitting in every sack. And I thought she had only me... well, there is Solokha for you!”"
